::SPEECH_U02_L02_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L02_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which statement is the best purpose for an informative speech?
{
~A. To confuse the audience with as many facts as possible.#This is not informative communication. Information should be selected and organized so the audience understands.
=B. To explain a topic clearly so the audience understands it better.#Correct. Informative speaking focuses on clear explanation and understanding.
~C. To entertain without teaching anything.#Entertainment may help delivery, but an informative speech must teach or explain.
~D. To argue a personal opinion without support.#That describes weak persuasion, not informative speech development.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L02_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L02_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.RES.02
Question:
Which sentence best explains reasoning after evidence?
{
~A. This evidence is here because I found it.#This does not explain how the evidence supports the point.
~B. Evidence is always correct.#Evidence still needs to be connected to the point through reasoning.
~C. I will now move to another topic.#This is a transition, not reasoning.
=D. This example matters because it shows how planning makes deadlines easier to see.#Correct. The sentence explains why the evidence supports the point.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L02_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L02_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which transition is most purposeful in an informative speech about study habits?
{
=A. After explaining how a planner organizes deadlines, the next step is to examine how review time supports memory.#Correct. This transition shows the connection between two study-habit ideas.
~B. Anyway, here is another thing.#This does not show how the ideas connect.
~C. My next idea is different and unrelated.#This tells the audience the speech is losing organization.
~D. I am done with that, so now this.#This is vague and does not guide the audience clearly.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L02_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L02_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which speech point best supports the focus "Organized notes help students study"?
{
~A. Some notebooks have colorful covers.#This detail may be true, but it does not explain how organized notes help studying.
~B. Students sometimes carry backpacks.#This is related to school but not to organized notes and studying.
=C. Headings help students find important sections quickly.#Correct. This point directly supports how organized notes help students study.
~D. School calendars include holidays.#This is unrelated to organized notes.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L02_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L02_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.RES.02
Question:
Which evidence best supports the point "Rehearsal improves speech timing"?
{
=A. A speaker who practices can notice if a section takes too long.#Correct. This evidence directly connects rehearsal to timing.
~B. Many students like group projects.#This does not support the point about rehearsal and timing.
~C. Some speeches happen in classrooms.#This is too general and does not prove rehearsal improves timing.
~D. Posters can be colorful.#This is unrelated to speech rehearsal.
}
